WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.460
Hi, I'm Nougat!

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:07.141
LongCat 2.0 is Meituan's open mixture of experts, with about forty-eight billion

00:00:07.141 --> 00:00:08.701
parameters active per token.

00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:11.041
Here's how its attention reads less.

00:00:12.392 --> 00:00:17.160
The idea: score the past cheaply, then read only a token-precise slice of it.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.226
Candidate blocks first, then the best tokens inside them.

00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:24.789
Under full attention, every query reads every past token.

00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:27.953
Over a long context, that is brutal to serve.

00:00:28.373 --> 00:00:30.197
Hierarchical indexing starts coarse.

00:00:30.197 --> 00:00:35.214
A cheap block-level pass proposes candidate blocks, the way MiniMax's sparse

00:00:35.214 --> 00:00:37.038
attention keeps whole blocks.

00:00:37.458 --> 00:00:41.938
Then a fine pass selects the most relevant individual tokens inside them.

00:00:41.938 --> 00:00:45.672
Token-precise, over a smaller set the indexer has to score.

00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:50.269
Its sparse attention attacks the indexer from three sides.

00:00:50.689 --> 00:00:54.064
Hierarchical indexing shrinks what the indexer has to score.

00:00:54.484 --> 00:00:59.973
Cross-layer indexing shares one index every two layers, since saliency is stable between neighbours.

00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:03.894
Half the layers reuse a selection instead of recomputing it.

00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:08.770
Streaming-aware indexing mixes contiguous reads with random picks, so the reads it

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.255
does issue are cheaper.

00:01:10.675 --> 00:01:14.742
One honest note: the public SGLang path drops the hierarchical stage, so

00:01:14.742 --> 00:01:17.114
what ships today is the other two.

00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:21.533
The usual way to add capacity is more experts.

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:26.535
Meituan says that past a sweet spot in sparsity, an n-gram embedding wins instead.

00:01:26.955 --> 00:01:31.579
So LongCat also carries a hundred and thirty-five billion parameters in an

00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:34.661
n-gram embedding: a lookup, orthogonal to the experts.

00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:39.698
Each move targets a distinct cost: what the indexer scores, how often

00:01:39.698 --> 00:01:41.895
it runs, and how cheaply it reads.

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:48.813
To recap: blocks then tokens, one index per two layers, and an

00:01:48.813 --> 00:01:50.582
n-gram lookup beside the experts.

00:01:51.002 --> 00:01:53.059
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:53.059 --> 00:01:53.647
I'm Nougat.

00:01:53.647 --> 00:01:53.941
Bye!
